    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d51">

                <pb facs="0055" n="43" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg43"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851G.00/5–2046: Circular telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Certain Diplomatic
                        Officers</hi><anchor n="48" xml:id="d51-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-05-20T08:00:00-04:00">May 20, 1946—8 a.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>D’Argenlieu to meet President Vietnam soon prior departure French, Vietnam delegs
                    Paris for final negotiations re governmental structure Indochina and Vietnam’s
                    status therein. French propose federal organization under High Commissioner who
                    exercise French Union’s powers and with Assembly initiate federal legislation.
                    Assembly composed 10 French members, 10 members each from Tonkin, Annam,
                    Cochinchina, Laos, Cambodia. Each state form own govt, elect Parliament, vote
                    own constitution, recruit defense army, fix budget, organize administration,
                    enact internal laws. Vietnam counter-proposal would limit federal powers to
                    customs policy currency and supplies coordination, would place relations between
                    Vietnam and federation on status international relations with High Commissioner
                    as ambassador to federation’s member states.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d51-f1" n="48" type="source" xml:id="d51-f1-content">
                <p>At Nanking and The Hague. This telegram was based on telegrams 160, May 14, 1946,
                    3 p.m., and 163, May 15, from Saigon, neither printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
